# AVAREN UX Principles

## Calm by default

- Generous spacing between elements and sections.
- One **primary action** per screen — everything else is secondary or hidden behind navigation.
- Important content before secondary detail.
- Long flows split into focused destinations, not endless scroll.

## Minimize taps

- Daily actions should begin in **two taps or fewer**.
- Preserve state across navigation (active workouts, timers, drafts).
- Save important changes immediately — never make the athlete confirm twice.

## Never clutter

- If a feature adds a permanent UI element, question whether it belongs.
- Prefer **removing UI** over adding UI.
- No feature flags, badges, or notification dots unless the user opted in.
- Modals are last resort; use dedicated screens for complex flows.

## Gym-ready

- Readable under **bright gym lighting** — high contrast, large tap targets.
- Timers never start automatically; athlete controls every transition.
- Fixed elements must not obscure navigation or workout controls.
- Local-first: workouts must work offline.

## AVA interaction

- AVA surfaces only on **explicit request** (tap, search, or dedicated entry).
- No interruptive toasts, coach marks, or "AI suggestions" on load.
- When AVA responds, present one focused answer — not a chat flood.

## Unified experience

Coaches and athletes share the same screens, navigation, and visual language. Coaching features appear as contextual panels or assignments — not a parallel app.

